---
import CopyButton from '../components/CopyButton.astro';
import SourceLink from '../components/SourceLink.astro';
import StatusTag from '../components/StatusTag.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getLatestPublishedRecord, matchRecordScope } from '../lib/catalog';
import discovery from '../../public/.well-known/mathgraph.json';

const published = await getLatestPublishedRecord('mg-l4yaml-source-check-20261010');
const { entry, record } = published;
const resolvePublished = await getLatestPublishedRecord('mg-living-warrant-openai-jpl-20261010');
const { entry: resolveEntry, record: resolveRecord } = resolvePublished;
const resolveOfflineRoot = `/evidence/${resolveEntry.id}/offline`;
const resolvePolicySha256 = '09e8d5ba97bca126a81dfe0fcbd5d57f3915ffe71402a497ece4d5e6117f2b4a';
const request = {
  record_id: entry.id,
  version: entry.version,
  source_commit: entry.scope.source_commit,
  external_suite_commit: entry.scope.external_suite_commit,
  goal: entry.scope.supported_goals[0],
};
const exactResult = matchRecordScope(entry, request);
const unsupportedResult = matchRecordScope(entry, { ...request, goal: 'whole_language_correctness' });
const sourceCatalogue = discovery.source_catalogue;
---

<BaseLayout title="MathGraph for agents — Exact, reusable evidence" description="Give an agent stable discovery routes, immutable evidence records, deterministic scope matching, and explicit UNKNOWN results." currentPath="/agents/">
  <header class="section section--compact">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="eyebrow">Agent interface</div>
      <h1>Point your agent to <span class="agent-route">mathgraph.org/agents</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">MathGraph gives agents source-pinned evidence they can cite without silently expanding its authority. Discovery is public and static; reuse requires exact structured scope matching.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button" href="/llms.txt">Open llms.txt</a>
        <a class="button button--secondary" href="/.well-known/mathgraph.json">Open discovery manifest</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell page-grid">
      <aside class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <strong>On this page</strong>
        <a href="#discover">Discover</a>
        <a href="#source-catalogue">Source catalogue</a>
        <a href="#modules">Modular contracts</a>
        <a href="#match">Record 001</a>
        <a href="#replay">Record 002</a>
        <a href="#integrity">Integrity</a>
        <a href="#limits">Current limits</a>
      </aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <section id="discover">
          <div class="eyebrow">01 · Discover</div>
          <h2>Start with a manifest, not a claim.</h2>
          <ol>
            <li>Read <a href="/llms.txt"><code>/llms.txt</code></a> or <a href="/.well-known/mathgraph.json"><code>/.well-known/mathgraph.json</code></a>.</li>
            <li>Fetch the short-lived <a href="/records/index.json"><code>/records/index.json</code></a> catalogue and record the local fetch time.</li>
            <li>Select an explicit record version and fetch its immutable JSON.</li>
            <li>Validate the schema and both the canonical and transport digests.</li>
            <li>Cite the permanent versioned page and content digest.</li>
          </ol>
          <p><code>generated_at</code> is publication time, not a response timestamp. Treat lifecycle state as fresh for at most <code>max_age_seconds</code> after your own fetch time, then re-fetch the catalogue.</p>
          <SourceLink href={entry.record_page_url} label="Permanent L4YAML record" meta={`${entry.id} · ${entry.version_slug}`} />
        </section>

        <section id="source-catalogue" data-agent-source-catalogue>
          <div class="eyebrow">02 · Consume source structure</div>
          <h2>Index source without promoting it into proof.</h2>
          <p>The Mathlib catalogue publishes real declaration occurrences and three closed-endpoint structural relations: <code>DECLARES</code>, <code>IMPORTS_MODULE</code>, and <code>TYPE_REFERENCES</code>. These are structural relations, never logical implication or semantic equivalence.</p>
          <ol data-agent-source-sequence>
            <li data-agent-source-step="1">Independently pin the exact <a href={sourceCatalogue.artifacts.manifest.url}>manifest</a> and <a href={sourceCatalogue.artifacts.trust_policy.url}>trust-policy</a> digests. The exact Git commit is one chosen trust channel, not truth.</li>
            <li data-agent-source-step="2">Hash the manifest bytes before parsing them.</li>
            <li data-agent-source-step="3">Validate the catalogue ID and version plus the Mathlib repository, commit, tree, and Lean toolchain pins.</li>
            <li data-agent-source-step="4">Fetch only manifest-listed search and shard paths, then verify every declared size and SHA-256 digest.</li>
            <li data-agent-source-step="5">Recompute object and relation identities, require closed endpoints, and admit only the three declared structural relation types.</li>
            <li data-agent-source-step="6">For an unsupported version, unknown critical extension, forged status, or missing endpoint: decline. Do not infer a nearby result.</li>
            <li data-agent-source-step="7">Return the authority-free result below. Do not convert source indexing into verification or action permission.</li>
          </ol>
          <dl class="module-artifacts" data-agent-source-result>
            <div><dt>Source status</dt><dd><code>{sourceCatalogue.result.source_status}</code></dd></div>
            <div><dt>Independent verification</dt><dd><code>{sourceCatalogue.result.independent_verification}</code></dd></div>
            <div><dt>Current applicability</dt><dd><code>{sourceCatalogue.result.current_applicability}</code></dd></div>
            <div><dt>Freshness</dt><dd><code>{sourceCatalogue.result.freshness}</code></dd></div>
            <div><dt>Authority</dt><dd><code>{sourceCatalogue.result.authority}</code></dd></div>
            <div><dt>Authorization</dt><dd><code>{sourceCatalogue.result.authorization}</code></dd></div>
          </dl>
          <div class="source-stack">
            <SourceLink href={sourceCatalogue.artifacts.consumer.url} label="Independent catalogue consumer" meta={`SHA-256 ${sourceCatalogue.artifacts.consumer.sha256}`} />
            <SourceLink href={sourceCatalogue.artifacts.trust_policy.url} label="Catalogue trust policy" meta={`SHA-256 ${sourceCatalogue.artifacts.trust_policy.sha256}`} />
            <SourceLink href={sourceCatalogue.artifacts.readme.url} label="Offline replay instructions" meta={`Public policy qualified by wrapper ${sourceCatalogue.qualification.public_bundle.wrapper_commit.slice(0, 12)}…`} />
          </div>
          <p>The exact public catalogue and trust policy were replayed by <a href={sourceCatalogue.qualification.public_bundle.run_url}>qualification run {sourceCatalogue.qualification.public_bundle.run_url.split('/').at(-1)} ↗</a>, then committed at <code>{sourceCatalogue.qualification.publication_commit}</code>. A later <a href={sourceCatalogue.qualification.later_regeneration.run_url}>regeneration run ↗</a> reproduced the catalogue publication with its own run-specific policy; it did not replay the public trust-policy bytes.</p>
          <p>A digest served by the same origin detects changed bytes relative to a trusted pin; it does not authenticate a compromised origin or establish mathematical truth. Pin the manifest and trust-policy digests through an independently chosen channel. Repository authentication is a trust-policy choice, not mathematical truth.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="modules">
          <h2>Choose a module without expanding its authority.</h2>
          <p>Record 001 is an exact-scope consumption contract. Record 002 is a historical offline replay contract. They are independently usable and neither grants authority to browser or map exports: those presentation files declare <code>authority: NONE</code>.</p>
          <p><a href="/discover/">Discover</a> is a local advisory contract. Its real source-pack result is <code>PARTIAL</code>, with <code>advisory_only: true</code>, <code>can_promote_truth: false</code>, verification <code>NOT_CHECKED</code>, and <code>FULL_REPLAY_UNAVAILABLE</code>. Attention is allocation, not truth confidence.</p>
          <p><a href="/develop/">Develop</a> has one exact eight-state <code>QUALIFIED_BOUNDED</code> profile from same-owner implementation-independent replay. The Capability Family Compiler remains <code>NOT_IMPLEMENTED</code>. VDN / Lean remains an unqualified <code>EXPERIMENTAL</code> candidate.</p>
          <p>The <a href="/capabilities/">Capabilities registry</a> has one qualified finite entry with exact contract, evaluation, receipt, and policy pins. Read the <a href="/capabilities/index-v1.json">registry JSON</a> and independently admit its policy and replay boundary. Reading the registry alone grants no trust. Current applicability is <code>UNKNOWN</code>; freshness, revocation, and supersession are <code>NOT_CHECKED</code>; action authorization is <code>NONE</code>.</p>
          <p><a href="/developers/#interoperability">External interoperability</a> is unsigned correspondence only. An in-toto wrapper creates no qualification or authentication. Modules are independently usable; no autonomous loop or compulsory pipeline is provided.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="match">
          <div class="eyebrow">03 · Match</div>
          <h2>Record 001 — exact source-scope match</h2>
          <p>An in-scope request must match the exact record ID, version, source pin, suite pin, and supported goal. This is a structured comparison—not an LLM judgment. Any mismatch or unsupported claim returns <StatusTag label="UNKNOWN" tone="unknown" />.</p>
          <div class="code-block"><div class="code-label">Exact request</div><pre tabindex="0"><code>{JSON.stringify(request, null, 2)}</code></pre></div>
          <div class="code-block"><div class="code-label">Exact result</div><pre tabindex="0"><code>{JSON.stringify(exactResult, null, 2)}</code></pre></div>
          <div class="code-block"><div class="code-label">Unsupported whole-language claim</div><pre tabindex="0"><code>{JSON.stringify(unsupportedResult, null, 2)}</code></pre></div>
        </section>

        <section id="replay">
          <div class="eyebrow">04 · Replay dependencies</div>
          <h2>Record 002 — offline dependency replay</h2>
          <p>This contract is different from record 001’s exact scope lookup. Download the immutable bundle, the separately supplied trust policy, and the standard-library Python consumer. Supply the expected policy digest through a trusted channel; the consumer validates typed claims, receipts, routes, withdrawals, and pins, then independently recomputes the seven historical outcomes.</p>
          <div class="source-stack">
            <SourceLink href={`${resolveOfflineRoot}/consumer.py`} label="Offline Python consumer" meta="Standard library · no producer import" />
            <SourceLink href={`${resolveOfflineRoot}/bundle.json`} label="Immutable replay bundle" meta={`Record ${resolveEntry.version_slug} · source-pinned inputs`} />
            <SourceLink href={`${resolveOfflineRoot}/trust-policy.json`} label="Resolve trust policy" meta={`Expected SHA-256 ${resolvePolicySha256.slice(0, 12)}…`} />
            <SourceLink href={resolveEntry.record_page_url} label="Permanent Resolve record" meta={`${resolveEntry.id} · ${resolveEntry.version_slug}`} />
          </div>
          <p>The replay establishes a pinned historical snapshot. Current applicability remains <code>{resolveEntry.current_applicability}</code>; freshness remains <code>{resolveEntry.freshness}</code>. It does not rerun the original JPL Lean proof. This consumer and the earlier RealityGraph comparison are same-owner independent implementations, not outside-organization adoption.</p>
          <p>The browser import report and knowledge-map download are presentation projections with authority <code>NONE</code>. They cannot be supplied as evidence to either independent consumer.</p>
          <p><strong>Snapshot digest</strong></p>
          <div class="copy-row"><code>{resolveRecord.snapshot_sha256}</code><CopyButton value={resolveRecord.snapshot_sha256} label="Copy Resolve digest" /></div>
        </section>

        <section id="integrity">
          <div class="eyebrow">05 · Verify identity</div>
          <h2>A digest is integrity, not authenticity.</h2>
          <p>The catalogue and record digest can detect changed bytes. If both arrive from a compromised host, the digest alone cannot authenticate that host or establish who published the replacement. Same-origin digests provide integrity relative to a trusted pin, not authenticity. The discovery manifest therefore links the committed catalogue source on GitHub as a second-origin provenance check, currently marked <code>UNSIGNED_PROVENANCE_ONLY</code>.</p>
          <p>A future signature must name a key policy and authenticate a specific statement. It still would not establish semantic truth.</p>
          <p><strong>Canonical content SHA-256</strong></p>
          <div class="copy-row"><code>{record.content_sha256}</code><CopyButton value={record.content_sha256} label="Copy digest" /></div>
          <p><strong>Source pin</strong></p>
          <div class="copy-row"><code>{record.source.commit}</code><CopyButton value={record.source.commit} label="Copy source pin" /></div>
          <p><strong>Suite pin</strong></p>
          <div class="copy-row"><code>{record.source.external_suite_commit}</code><CopyButton value={record.source.external_suite_commit} label="Copy suite pin" /></div>
        </section>

        <section id="limits">
          <div class="eyebrow">06 · Limits</div>
          <h2>The static interface does not execute claims.</h2>
          <div class="callout callout--unknown">
            <StatusTag label="Current boundary" tone="unknown" />
            <p>The qualified <code>/v1/resolve</code> contract remains a local/preview interface. A public resolver is not deployed. An MCP endpoint is not deployed. Discover and Develop are local contracts with static documentation; their hosted services remain <code>NOT_DEPLOYED</code>. The capability registry is static. MathGraph.org accepts no visitor-supplied code, proof, claim, or URL.</p>
          </div>
          <p>Whole-language YAML correctness, natural-language fidelity, generalization beyond the pinned cases, and newer source revisions remain <code>UNKNOWN</code>.</p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
